---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BatesNumberingPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf/BatesNumberingPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="bates-numbering-pdf">
  <BatesNumberingPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>What Bates numbering is for</h2>
      <p>
        In litigation, a document production can have tens of thousands of pages in hundreds of files.
        Bates numbering gives each page a unique, sequential, permanent identifier such as
        <code>ABC000123</code>. A deposition transcript, a privilege log and a motion can then cite the
        same page without doubt, years later, across parties that do not share a file system.
      </p>
      <p>
        Patent filings, clinical trial documentation, audit evidence, insurance claims and
        construction submittals use sequential page stamping for the same reason.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Choosing a format</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Element</th>
            <th scope="col">Convention</th>
            <th scope="col">Example</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Prefix</td>
            <td>Abbreviation of the party or producing entity, 2–5 letters</td>
            <td><code>SMITH</code>, <code>DEF</code>, <code>ACME</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Digits</td>
            <td>Fixed width, wide enough for the highest number</td>
            <td><code>000001</code>: six digits, one million pages</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Suffix</td>
            <td>Optional confidentiality designation</td>
            <td><code>-CONF</code>, <code>-AEO</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Position</td>
            <td>Bottom right is almost universal</td>
            <td><code>ACME000042-CONF</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Never change the digit count during a production. Numbers sort as text, so
        <code>ABC9999</code> and <code>ABC10000</code> sort in the wrong order, but
        <code>ABC009999</code> and <code>ABC010000</code> sort correctly. Pick a width that covers the
        whole matter and keep it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Continuous numbering across files</h2>
      <p>
        Add all files in production order (drag the rows to reorder them) and leave continuous
        numbering on. File two starts at the number after the last page of file one, file three after
        that, and so on. The result is one unbroken range across the whole set, which a production
        letter must describe.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tool lists each output file with its first and last stamp. The CSV log records each page:
        file name, page number in that file, and the Bates number assigned. Attach that log to a
        production index or load it into a review platform.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The white backing box</h2>
      <p>
        A stamp in the bottom-right corner of a dense page can fall on a footer, a page number or a
        table rule and become unreadable. The backing box paints an opaque white rectangle a little
        larger than the text before the number is drawn, so the number is always legible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Turn it off when the corner is always blank and you do not want to cover anything, or when the
        formatting rules of a court forbid changes to the underlying image.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Real text, not a picture</h2>
      <p>
        Each stamp is PDF text in one of the fourteen fonts built into the format. The number is
        selectable, copyable and searchable in all readers, stays sharp at all zoom levels, and adds a
        few dozen bytes per page. Review platforms that index the text layer find the Bates numbers
        automatically.
      </p>
      <p>
        For pages with a rotation flag, the stamp is rotated to match. It appears upright in the corner
        that the reader sees, not sideways in the corner of the raw coordinates.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why doing this locally matters here more than anywhere</h2>
      <p>
        Documents that need Bates numbering are documents in a legal dispute: privileged
        communications, medical records, financial statements, source code. Uploading them to a
        third-party web service is a confidentiality problem, and in some jurisdictions one that must
        be disclosed.
      </p>
      <p>
        This page does not transmit the files. pdf-lib does the stamping in your browser, and it works
        when you disconnect from the network first. The design makes uploads impossible; this does not
        depend on a policy.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
